{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791088434,"nonce":"Jkush97MNblJT1+fU5Wv8qS/WMU=","title":"haxe.Exception.stack is a String, not the upstream CallStack property","message":"rayzor's own `compiler/haxe-std/haxe/Exception.hx` declares `public var stack:String`, filled at the throw site (statements.rs, `rayzor_native_stack_trace_call_stack`). Upstream Haxe 4 has `stack(get,never):CallStack`; Haxe 5 has `stack(get,set):CallStack`. Haxe 5's vendored `sys/thread/Thread.hx` already calls `haxe.CallStack.toString(e.stack)`.\n\nIssue12213 assigns an `Array\u003cStackItem\u003e` to `e.stack` and compares `Std.string(stack)` with `Std.string(e.stack)`. At base it passes only because both sides are mangled the same way. Once cross-module calls box concrete arguments for Dynamic formals (the TestDeque fix), the String-typed field is boxed as a String and the comparison fails. That is a false pass lost, not a regression.\n\nTried: `stack(get,set):CallStack` backed by `__nativeStack:String` (throw site writes it) and a lazily converted `__stack`. It works in isolation, but:\n- `Exception.hx` then imports `CallStack`, and `CallStack` imports `Exception`. The import cycle reorders stdlib compilation. `PosException` lowered before `haxe.Exception` (bare `extends Exception` became a Placeholder) and `NotImplementedException` inherited no `stack`.\n- With that ordering fixed, Issue4094 crashed: inside Xml.insertChild, `x.parent.children.remove(x)` lowered via try_user_class_method_call (symbol \"-\") into BalancedTree.remove instead of the extern Array path. The order-dependent receiver typing is the real bug there.\n- `NativeStackTrace.toHaxe` is a runtime stub returning `[]` (native_stack_trace.rs), so a real `e.stack` would be empty anyway.\n\nBlocks Issue12213. Also depends on the array-of-enum printing issue.","files":null}]}